cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use genodiff in your research, please cite it."
title: "genodiff: a self-contained, zero-dependency tool for whole-genome alignment and variant calling between haploid genome assemblies"
authors:
  - family-names: "Zhao"
    given-names: "Haijun"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
version: "1.2.0"
date-released: "2026-10-19"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/<your-username>/genodiff"
abstract: >-
  genodiff is a self-contained whole-genome comparison tool written from
  scratch in Rust. It aligns haploid genome assemblies against a reference
  using a canonical minimizer index and greedy collinear chaining with
  patience-based break tolerance, calls SNPs and small InDels via banded
  affine gap alignment, infers structural variants (DEL, INS, INV, DUP, BND)
  from chain topology, and outputs standard VCF 4.2. The tool has zero
  external dependencies and processes 800 Mb genomes in under 2 minutes.
keywords:
  - bioinformatics
  - genome assembly comparison
  - structural variant calling
  - minimizer
  - synteny
  - Rust
